  <article class="prose max-w-none mt-8">
    <p class="lead text-lg">Nestled on Bali's northeast coast, under the watchful eye of the majestic Mount Agung, lies the tranquil village of Tulamben. Once a quiet fishing community, it is now a world-renowned mecca for scuba divers. Home to the iconic <strong>USAT Liberty Wreck</strong>, Tulamben offers an astonishing diversity of underwater experiences, from vibrant coral gardens and dramatic walls to a treasure trove of macro critters hiding in the volcanic black sand.</p>
    
    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#23a0b4] pl-4 italic text-gray-600 my-6">
      “Tulamben is where history sleeps and the ocean comes alive. Every dive reveals a new secret, a new creature, a new reason to fall in love with Bali's underwater world.”
    </blockquote>

    <h2>The Legendary USAT Liberty Wreck</h2>
    <p>The crown jewel of Tulamben is, without a doubt, the USAT Liberty. This 120-meter American cargo ship was torpedoed by a Japanese submarine in 1942 and beached at Tulamben. In 1963, tremors from the eruption of Mount Agung pushed the vessel back into the sea, creating one of the most accessible and spectacular wreck dives on the planet.
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Incredible Marine Life:</strong> The wreck is now a thriving artificial reef, completely colonized by coral and home to an astounding density of fish. Witness the resident school of bumphead parrotfish on an early morning dive, get enveloped by a vortex of big-eyed trevallies, and spot giant barracuda and sea turtles.</li>
      <li><strong>For Every Diver:</strong> Lying just 30 meters from the shore, the wreck starts at a shallow 5 meters and slopes down to 30 meters, making it perfect for try dives, Open Water courses, fun divers, and underwater photographers.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond the Wreck: Tulamben's Other Gems</h2>
    <p>While the Liberty gets most of the attention, Tulamben's other sites are equally captivating and offer completely different experiences.</p>
    
    <h3>The Drop-Off (Tulamben Wall)</h3>
    <p>At the opposite end of the bay from the wreck lies a dramatic volcanic wall, formed by an ancient lava flow from Mount Agung. The wall plummets to depths of over 60 meters and is adorned with enormous gorgonian sea fans, sponges, and hard corals. It's a fantastic multi-level dive where you can often spot larger marine life like reef sharks and Napoleon wrasse, while the intricate wall structure hides moray eels, lionfish, and countless crustaceans.</p>

    <h3>Coral Garden</h3>
    <p>Situated between the Liberty Wreck and the Drop-Off, this is a beautiful and relaxed shallow dive site. Perfect for beginners, macro photographers, and a second or third dive of the day. The site is a vibrant carpet of anemones hosting various species of clownfish (Nemo!). You can also find ribbon eels, scorpionfish, and a wealth of interesting critters. It’s also an excellent spot for snorkeling.</p>
    
    <h3>Seraya Secrets: A Macro Photographer's Paradise</h3>
    <p>Just a short drive from the main Tulamben bay, this black sand "muck diving" site is a must for photographers and anyone who loves finding weird and wonderful critters. Here, our expert guides will help you spot some of Bali's most sought-after macro subjects, including harlequin shrimp, boxer crabs, ghost pipefish, and an array of rare nudibranchs.</p>

    <h2>Plan Your Tulamben Dive with Bali Diving</h2>
    <p>At Bali Diving, we specialize in creating exceptional underwater adventures. Our PADI 5-Star rating reflects our commitment to safety, quality, and personalized service. Our experienced local guides have an intimate knowledge of Tulamben's dive sites and an incredible eye for spotting marine life, ensuring you see the best of what this magical place has to offer.</p>
    <p>We provide comfortable, air-conditioned transport from South Bali, top-of-the-line rental equipment, and a delicious lunch by the sea. All you need to do is relax and enjoy the world-class diving. Ready to explore the underwater heart of Bali? Book your Tulamben trip with us today!</p>
  </article>

